  "text": "Question seven. What is clustering, and how does it differ from partitioning? Clustering sorts rows inside storage, so the engine can skip blocks that can't match a filter. In the sales file, each block of rows records its first and last date. In date order, March overlaps two blocks of nine. Shuffled, every block spans the year, and the same query read five times the bytes. Partitions are coarse, a few large segments, often with a cap on how many. Clustering is fine-grained, and handles columns with many values. So partition by date, and cluster by the high-cardinality keys your filters use.\n\nQuestion eight. When can partitioning or clustering hurt more than help? When the partitions are tiny. Split the million sales by day, and you get three hundred sixty-six files of about twenty-seven kilobytes. Their footers alone come to two hundred six kilobytes, against under five for one file, and reading the year opens every file. And clustering on a column no query filters buys nothing, while the sorting still costs maintenance. Partition and cluster on the columns filters actually use, and match the grain to the volume: a million rows a year is too few to split by day.",
